{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KLdLzsj8U1aCHKaF7n8Tmu","type":"tool_result","content":"<system-reminder>This memory is 8 days old. Memories are point-in-time observations, not live state — claims about code behavior or file:line citations may be outdated. Verify against current code before asserting as fact.</system-reminder>\n1\t---\n2\tname: worker-agents-background-full-suite\n3\tdescription: \"The shell tool caps one call at 600s, so ai-template's ~20-min full pytest run ALWAYS auto-converts to background in worker subagents; expect a wait/resume dance or direct the batched form.\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: feedback\n7\t  originSessionId: bfd9ad42-7a63-4a20-b97c-5b8f19ca85ab\n8\t  modified: 2026-08-23T01:30:44.560Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tIn ai-template, the full suite (`python -m pytest -q -n auto --basetemp=D:\\pytest-tmp`) takes ~17–19 min, but the Bash/PowerShell tool hard-caps a single call at 600 s and AUTO-CONVERTS it to background past that — \"run it foreground\" is physically impossible in one call, for the orchestrator and for worker subagents alike (learned 2026-08-22, WI-503/WI-504 workers; the workers were not disobeying their briefs).\n12\t\n13\t**What actually happens:** the run keeps going in background; the agent ends its turn \"waiting\"; a completion notification later re-invokes it and it finishes the close (worked for WI-503). One resume nudge may still be needed if the agent mis-times its wait.\n14\t\n15\t**How to apply:** either accept the auto-background + notification cycle (budget one extra round-trip per full-suite worker and don't scold the worker for it), or direct the BATCHED form docs/status.md already names for this box: split the suite into <10-min chunks run foreground (e.g. two halves by test directory, same basetemp hygiene, quote both totals). Never treat a \"waiting for the suite\" stop as a fabrication risk — the worker is doing the honest thing. Related: [[ai-template-complexity-audit]].\n16\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28a5f524-44ec-4973-a9db-b50119b38249","uuid":"5ba32283-5522-4f2a-9d54-e7ba0b4cc49b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:39:05.736Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Peter\\.claude\\projects\\C--Projects-ai-template\\memory\\worker-agents-background-full-suite.md","content":"---\nname: worker-agents-background-full-suite\ndescription: \"The shell tool caps one call at 600s, so ai-template's ~20-min full pytest run ALWAYS auto-converts to background in worker subagents; expect a wait/resume dance or direct the batched form.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: bfd9ad42-7a63-4a20-b97c-5b8f19ca85ab\n  modified: 2026-08-23T01:30:44.560Z\n---\n\nIn ai-template, the full suite (`python -m pytest -q -n auto --basetemp=D:\\pytest-tmp`) takes ~17–19 min, but the Bash/PowerShell tool hard-caps a single call at 600 s and AUTO-CONVERTS it to background past that — \"run it foreground\" is physically impossible in one call, for the orchestrator and for worker subagents alike (learned 2026-08-22, WI-503/WI-504 workers; the workers were not disobeying their briefs).\n\n**What actually happens:** the run keeps going in background; the agent ends its turn \"waiting\"; a completion notification later re-invokes it and it finishes the close (worked for WI-503). One resume nudge may still be needed if the agent mis-times its wait.\n\n**How to apply:** either accept the auto-background + notification cycle (budget one extra round-trip per full-suite worker and don't scold the worker for it), or direct the BATCHED form docs/status.md already names for this box: split the suite into <10-min chunks run foreground (e.g. two halves by test directory, same basetemp hygiene, quote both totals). Never treat a \"waiting for the suite\" stop as a fabrication risk — the worker is doing the honest thing. Related: [[ai-template-complexity-audit]].\n","numLines":16,