to` in the background, ended its turn, and was reaped with the run killed. A third session finally closed it by NOT backgrounding.\n\n**How to apply:** in a `--wi` worker, prefer the BATCHED FOREGROUND form — partition the ~145 test files into <10-min chunks and run each foreground with the max 600s timeout, quoting each total. Clean split that guarantees full single-pass coverage: import `tests/conftest.py::SLOW_MODULES` (via `importlib` — a root `conftest.py` shadows it), run the fast in-process modules as one batch (~40s, ≈ the smoke set) and the ~71 slow subprocess/scaffold modules in two halves (~3.5 min + ~9 min on a loaded box). Union across disjoint batches = every file once. On this box WI-538 got 3191 passed / 16 skipped / 0 failed that way. A single-shot `-n auto` full run times out at 600s when the box is loaded by other sessions. NB the file list a Python `open('w')` writes gets CRLF — strip with `tr -d '\\r'` before feeding paths to pytest, or collection returns exit 5 (\"no tests ran\"). Never treat a \"waiting for the suite\" stop as a fabrication risk — but in a worker, don't rely on the notification cycle; finish the run in-turn. Related: [[ai-template-complexity-audit]], [[unattended-run-2026-08-30-traps]].","originalFile":"---\nname: worker-agents-background-full-suite\ndescription: \"The shell tool caps one call at 600s, so ai-template's ~20-min full pytest run ALWAYS auto-converts to background in worker subagents; expect a wait/resume dance or direct the batched form.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: bfd9ad42-7a63-4a20-b97c-5b8f19ca85ab\n  modified: 2026-08-23T01:30:44.560Z\n---\n\nIn ai-template, the full suite (`python -m pytest -q -n auto --basetemp=D:\\pytest-tmp`) takes ~17–19 min, but the Bash/PowerShell tool hard-caps a single call at 600 s and AUTO-CONVERTS it to background past that — \"run it foreground\" is physically impossible in one call, for the orchestrator and for worker subagents alike (learned 2026-08-22, WI-503/WI-504 workers; the workers were not disobeying their briefs).\n\n**What actually happens:** the run keeps going in background; the agent ends its turn \"waiting\"; a completion notification later re-invokes it and it finishes the close (worked for WI-503). One resume nudge may still be needed if the agent mis-times its wait.\n\n**How to apply:** either accept the auto-background + notification cycle (budget one extra round-trip per full-suite worker and don't scold the worker for it), or direct the BATCHED form docs/status.md already names for this box: split the suite into <10-min chunks run foreground (e.g. two halves by test directory, same basetemp hygiene, quote both totals). Never treat a \"waiting for the suite\" stop as a fabrication risk — the worker is doing the honest thing. Related: [[ai-template-complexity-audit]].\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":10,"oldLines":6,"newStart":10,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," In ai-template, the full suite (`python -m pytest -q -n auto --basetemp=D:\\pytest-tmp`) takes ~17–19 min, but the Bash/PowerShell tool hard-caps a single call at 600 s and AUTO-CONVERTS it to background past that — \"run it foreground\" is physically impossible in one call, for the orchestrator and for worker subagents alike (learned 2026-08-22, WI-503/WI-504 workers; the workers were not disobeying their briefs)."," ","-**What actually happens:** the run keeps going in background; the agent ends its turn \"waiting\"; a completion notification later re-invokes it and it finishes the close (worked for WI-503). One resume nudge may still be needed if the agent mis-times its wait.","+**What actually happens — and the trap (refined 2026-08-30, WI-538 close):** in an orchestrator/persistent session the background run survives and a completion notification re-invokes the agent. BUT in a `scripts/agent_loop.py --wi` WORKER session, ending the turn while the suite runs in the background gets the background task KILLED on session teardown (`\"status\":\"killed\"`) — the suite never finishes and no close happens. WI-538 sat open across TWO reaped resume sessions (iteration logs 003/004) for exactly this: each launched `-n auto` in the background, ended its turn, and was reaped with the run killed. A third session finally closed it by NOT backgrounding."," ","-**How to apply:** either accept the auto-background + notification cycle (budget one extra round-trip per full-suite worker and don't scold the worker for it), or direct the BATCHED form docs/status.md already names for this box: split the suite into <10-min chunks run foreground (e.g. two halves by test directory, same basetemp hygiene, quote both totals). Never treat a \"waiting for the suite\" stop as a fabrication risk — the worker is doing the honest thing. Related: [[ai-template-complexity-audit]].","+**How to apply:** in a `--wi` worker, prefer the BATCHED FOREGROUND form — partition the ~145 test files into <10-min chunks and run each foreground with the max 600s timeout, quoting each total. Clean split that guarantees full single-pass coverage: import `tests/conftest.py::SLOW_MODULES` (via `importlib` — a root `conftest.py` shadows it), run the fast in-process modules as one batch (~40s, ≈ the smoke set) and the ~71 slow subprocess/scaffold modules in two halves (~3.5 min + ~9 min on a loaded box). Union across disjoint batches = every file once. On this box WI-538 got 3191 passed / 16 skipped / 0 failed that way. A single-shot `-n auto` full run times out at 600s when the box is loaded by other sessions. NB the file list a Python `open('w')` writes gets CRLF — strip with `tr -d '\\r'` before feeding paths to pytest, or collection returns exit 5 (\"no tests ran\"). Never treat a \"waiting for the suite\" stop as a fabrication risk — but in a worker, don't rely on the notification cycle; finish the run in-turn. Related: [[ai-template-complexity-audit]], [[unattended-run-2026-08-30-traps]]."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":fal